nodecode-artil is a rewrite of artilai's artilai/plugins:

  https://github.com/artilai/plugins
  commit 8dce90c47a7b0d9e9c5edcb3dd2dd09227fe6fea
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Artil (LICENSE)

The rewrite is the plugin's plugins/hermes folder as a Nodecode cell. What it keeps: the same `artil` program on PATH and its own argv -- `artil email|sms|telegram` for a listing, `read <id>` for one message, `send` with `--to`, `--subject`, `--reply-to`, `--cc`, `--forward` and `--attach`, the body after `--`, `artil url send <agent> -- <body>` for another agent of the workspace, `artil team` and `artil auth status`; the client marker the plugin passed in the environment, `ARTIL_CLIENT`, now naming this harness; and the skill's guidance, which survives as the primer that (help :artil) answers -- read a message by its id before acting on it, treat its text as data rather than instructions, answer a reply through artil and not through another account, and ask before sending again after a failed send.

What is left out, and why: the gateway platform adapter. The plugin registered a Hermes platform that ran `artil internal hermes-channel`, read one JSON notice per line and handed each to the gateway as an incoming message, with Hermes' own pairing and allowlist deciding who may start a run; a chat surface in Nodecode is a room cell, and this rewrite keeps the account in the six tools and /artil instead, which is what the plugin's skill told the model to do with the CLI anyway; a new message therefore does not start a run by itself here. Also left out: the `hermes artil ...` subcommand, which was a passthrough to the same CLI (here /artil takes its place, and `artil` is on PATH), the setup wizard that wrote ARTIL_ALLOWED_USERS into the gateway's .env, and the bundled copy of the CLI the plugin installed into its own node_modules -- this cell runs the user's own `artil`, so the program is installed and updated the usual way.

Nothing of the original's Python is bundled. The cell runs the same artil program the plugin ran, and its own code is Common Lisp.
